  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "b1",
      "function": "hook",
      "scene_type": "gb_task",
      "spoken": "People say you can see the Great Wall of China from space. Is that true? NASA has a clear answer.",
      "data": {
        "kicker": "Check · Great Wall of China",
        "question": "Can you see the Wall from space?",
        "question_at": 0.05,
        "tag": {
          "text": "Claim: visible from space",
          "at": "@true",
          "y": 1010
        }
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b2",
      "function": "problem",
      "scene_type": "gb_principle",
      "spoken": "It is often called the only structure you can see from space. NASA calls that a myth. From the Moon it is not visible, and from orbit it is hard or impossible to see with the naked eye.",
      "data": {
        "headline": "The finding",
        "note": "according to NASA",
        "rule": {
          "text": "Moon: no. Orbit: barely, naked eye.",
          "at": "@Moon",
          "dy": 640
        }
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S1",
        "S2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b3",
      "function": "explanation",
      "scene_type": "gb_load",
      "spoken": "Distance is not the only reason. What matters is contrast, NASA writes. The wall barely stands out from the plants and soil around it. That is why satellite sensors pick it out more easily than the human eye.",
      "data": {
        "headline": "The reason",
        "load": {
          "label": "What matters",
          "value": "Contrast",
          "unit": "",
          "sub": "not just the distance",
          "at": "@contrast",
          "dy": 540
        },
        "goal": {
          "key": "IMAGE",
          "text": "The wall barely stands out",
          "at": "@plants",
          "dy": 420
        },
        "pins": [
          {
            "anchor": "wall",
            "text": "Wall",
            "at": "@wall",
            "side": "up"
          }
        ]
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b4",
      "function": "demonstration",
      "scene_type": "gb_proof",
      "spoken": "With a telephoto lens it is a different story. Leroy Chiao, commander of Expedition 10, photographed the wall from the space station. It can also be made out on some high-resolution astronaut photos. So the picture was taken with a long lens, not with the naked eye.",
      "data": {
        "headline": "The check",
        "stamp": {
          "date": "EXPEDITION 10",
          "event": "Photo from the ISS",
          "at": "@Expedition",
          "dy": 150
        },
        "pins": [
          {
            "anchor": "far",
            "text": "Telephoto: yes",
            "at": "@photographed",
            "side": "up"
          }
        ]
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S1",
        "S2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b5",
      "function": "close",
      "scene_type": "gb_close",
      "spoken": "So: it can be photographed, but not seen with the naked eye. China's first astronaut, Yang Liwei, said he did not see the wall. So it is not invisible from space, but you cannot simply look down and spot it either. Follow Gigastruct for the next check.",
      "data": {
        "statement": "Verdict: not with the naked eye",
        "sheet": "SHEET 02",
        "rows": [
          [
            "Moon",
            "not visible"
          ],
          [
            "Orbit",
            "naked eye: barely"
          ],
          [
            "Camera",
            "telephoto: yes"
          ],
          [
            "Source",
            "NASA"
          ]
        ],
        "cta": {
          "text": "Follow Gigastruct",
          "at": "@Follow"
        }
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S2"
      ]
    }
